{
  "title": "iOS local search using the shared search-ranking vectors",
  "description": "Search relevance ranking is implemented server-side as a \
pure scoring function in ramekin-core/src/search.rs, with a JSON fixture \
corpus at ramekin-core/tests/fixtures/search_ranking/cases.json that is \
deliberately structured as shared test vectors (query tokens + recipe fields \
in, ranked IDs out). The long-term plan (discussed 2026-07-03) is for iOS to \
run the same matching + scoring locally for instant, offline search, with the \
vector file guaranteeing identical ranking to the server. Web stays \
server-side — it has no offline story and no local recipe corpus, so it \
consumes ranking via the API and cannot drift.\n\
\n\
Scope when unblocked:\n\
- Swift implementation of normalize_for_search (lowercase + strip combining \
marks, mirroring Postgres f_unaccent + ILIKE semantics) and relevance_score \
with the same field weights.\n\
- Swift implementation of the matching filter (every text token must appear in \
title/description/instructions/notes/ingredients, accent- and case-insensitive \
substring), which is currently SQL in server/src/api/recipes/list.rs.\n\
- XCTest that loads the shared vector corpus as a test resource (xcodegen \
project.yml resources entry) and asserts identical ranking; move/copy the \
corpus to shared-test-vectors/ per the conventions from \
p3-shared-test-vectors-recipe-scaling if that pilot has landed by then.\n\
- CI wiring: add the vector path to .github/workflows/ios.yml trigger paths so \
a vector edit cannot merge without running the Swift side.\n\
\n\
Per doc/client-logic-sharing.md this is the duplicate-and-pin pattern; do NOT \
reach for WASM/UniFFI for a ~100-line scorer.",
